A recommendation is a category-B-recommendation and is an evidence-type-4-recommendation.
If a clinician prescribes an initial-opioid-therapy to a patient for an acute-pain then the clinician should review a controlled-substance-prescription-history for the patient with a state-PDMP-record and should assess the patient for a high-overdose-risk from an opioid-dosage and should assess the patient for a high-overdose-risk from a medication-combination.
If a clinician prescribes an initial-opioid-therapy to a patient for a subacute-pain then the clinician should review a controlled-substance-prescription-history for the patient with a state-PDMP-record and should assess the patient for a high-overdose-risk from an opioid-dosage and should assess the patient for a high-overdose-risk from a medication-combination.
If a clinician prescribes an initial-opioid-therapy to a patient for a chronic-pain then the clinician should review a controlled-substance-prescription-history for the patient with a state-PDMP-record and should assess the patient for a high-overdose-risk from an opioid-dosage and should assess the patient for a high-overdose-risk from a medication-combination.
If a patient receives a chronic-opioid-therapy then every clinician should periodically review a controlled-substance-prescription-history for the patient with a state-PDMP-record during the chronic-opioid-therapy and should periodically assess the patient for a high-overdose-risk from an opioid-dosage and should periodically assess the patient for a high-overdose-risk from a medication-combination.
